  "body": "﻿A follow-up from the review of PR #3971.\n\nPR #3883 (merged) proved the link token without disclosure: the hello carries a siphash proof, the window answers its own proof, and the raw token never crosses the wire. This closes the wire-disclosure path.\n\n## What remains\n\nIssue #3874 names a local user who reads a stale discovery file. That user knows the token from the file, so they can compute every proof. The CLI learns a stale entry is stale only after a connect fails. A false server on the freed port of a crashed window answers and proves, and the CLI sends its tool calls to it.\n\nThe PR review of #3971 states the same limit: \"Use an authentication exchange that proves prior knowledge of the secret without disclosing it first, or use a transport that verifies the peer identity.\" A shared secret that both sides read from the file cannot separate the peers by cryptography on this transport.\n\n## Fixes to consider\n\n- Before the connect, check that the pid in the file is alive, and remove the file of a dead pid. This removes most stale entries. The design exists on branch issue/3874-link-proves-token (PR #3971).\n- On Windows, a named pipe with a user-only ACL replaces the TCP port. The CLI asks the kernel which process serves the pipe and refuses any other peer before the token proof. The full design, its tests, and two incidental bug fixes (a pipe-instance handle leak; overlapped waits on a NULL hEvent) are preserved on that branch and PR.\n\nSuggested labels: P3, area:mcp, effort:high, source:agent.\r\n",
